@[system]

※※ Files and directories, and what a program does its I/O with.
※
※ Imported by `std` and handed on, so a program knows it as `std.fs`.  The
※ devices themselves and the ring are `io`'s, which this imports: `State` keeps
※ the ring and the tasks `std.run` drives, a `Dir` is where files are opened,
※ and what `Dir.open` answers is a device of `io` carrying where the ring is.

let io := ⎕import("io")

@[external("pl4g_io_drain"), io]
fn io_drain(r: &mut io.Ring)

@[external("pl4g_io_step"), io]
fn io_step(r: &mut io.Ring)

@[external("pl4g_io_open"), io]
fn io_open(how: i32, dir: i32, at: u64, len: u64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_close"), io]
fn io_close(r: &mut io.Ring, fd: i32) → i64

※※ A task as `std.run` holds it: the walk, and the request it is waiting on --
※※ one whose slot is negative, before its first turn.
@[export]
type Task = walk : ⌖⇉io.Pending ⇇ io.Answer in ⎕heap ; waiting : io.Pending

※※ What a program does its I/O with: the ring, and the tasks `std.run` drives.
※※
※※ \details A device: a function handed one may read and write through it.  One
※※ of these is a field of what a program is started with, `init.fs`, and in the
※※ image: where it is does not change, so a device made from it carries that
※※ address and reaches the ring through it.  The ring's own descriptor is
※※ `ring.fd`, made by the runtime the first time a request is started.
※※
※※ `cwd` is the directory the process is in, one object for the whole program
※※ and read-only: its descriptor is `AT_FDCWD`, and its ring the program's, which
※※ the compiler fills in -- the one singleton written without a value.
@[export, device]
type State = ring : io.Ring ; tasks : [Task] ; singleton cwd : Dir

※※ A directory, to open files in -- and other directories.
※※
※※ \details `fd` is the directory's descriptor, or `AT_FDCWD` for the one the
※※ process is in; `ring` is where the ring is, which every file opened in it
※※ carries on.  One `Dir.open` answers is closed with `Dir.close`, by whatever
※※ holds it when its scope ends; the one the process is in is a singleton of
※※ `State`'s, reached by reference, and never closed.
@[export, device, closed_by(close)]
type Dir = fd : i32 ; ring : u64

※※ What the kernel said, where it refused, as it says it: a positive number,
※※ which is the number `⎕errno` would have been.
fn refusal(of: i64) → i32:
    ⎕narrow(0i64 - of, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32

※※ The directory the process is in, to open files relative to.
※※
※※ \return the one such directory the program has, `State.cwd`, whose
※※ descriptor is `AT_FDCWD` and which is never closed
@[export]
fn cwd() → &static Dir:
    &State.cwd

※※ A device made of a descriptor the program has from somewhere else.
※※
※※ \details A pipe `⎕syscall` made, one a parent handed down: what comes back
※※ reaches the ring `s` keeps, and is closed as one opened is.  Nothing is
※※ asked of the kernel, so nothing is refused.  Written
※※ `init.fs.adopt(fd, ⌜std.io.Writer⌝)`.
※※
※※ \param s what the program does its I/O with: `&init.fs`
※※ \param fd the descriptor
※※ \param kind which device to make: `⌜io.Reader⌝`, `⌜io.Writer⌝`,
※※ `⌜io.ReadWriter⌝` or `⌜Dir⌝`
※※ \return the device, which the caller closes
※
※ What a device carries besides its descriptor is where the ring is, and that is
※ `s`'s to say: written out, it is an address copied from some device the
※ program happens to hold.  Nothing is asked of the kernel -- the descriptor is
※ the program's word -- so nothing can be refused here.
@[export]
fn State.adopt(s: &State, fd: i32, kind: ⌜T'⌝) → T'
        pre(⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Reader⌝ ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Writer⌝ ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.ReadWriter⌝
            ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜Dir⌝):
    let ring: u64 = ⎕address(&s⌖.ring)
    comptime if ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Reader⌝:
        io.Reader(.fd ← fd, .ring ← ring)
    comptime elif ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Writer⌝:
        io.Writer(.fd ← fd, .ring ← ring)
    comptime elif ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.ReadWriter⌝:
        io.ReadWriter(.fd ← fd, .ring ← ring)
    else:
        Dir(.fd ← fd, .ring ← ring)

※※ Wait for the kernel to answer something -- anything: what `std.run` does
※※ when no task can go on.
@[export]
fn State.step(s: &mut State):
    io_step(&mut s⌖.ring)

※※ Wait for everything outstanding.  The entry point does this when the startup
※※ function has returned; nothing stops a program doing it sooner.
@[export]
fn State.drain(s: &mut State):
    io_drain(&mut s⌖.ring)

※※ Open the file `name` in `d`, for what the type handed says.
※※
※※ \details `⌜io.Reader⌝` to read it; `⌜io.Writer⌝` to write it, made if it is
※※ not there and emptied if it is; `⌜io.ReadWriter⌝` for both, made if it is
※※ not there and left as it is; and `⌜Dir⌝` for the directory `name`, to open
※※ files in, refused (`not_a_directory`) where it is none.  The name is relative
※※ to `d` unless it begins with `/`; one with a nul inside it or longer than
※※ 4095 bytes is refused before the kernel is asked.  Written through a value,
※※ `std.fs.cwd().open(name, ⌜std.io.Reader⌝)`.
※※
※※ \param d the directory `name` is relative to, where it is relative
※※ \param name the file
※※ \param kind which of the three to make
※※ \return the device, which the caller closes
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
※
※ The type is the argument, rather than a mode beside the name, because what
※ comes back is a different type for each: a `Reader` cannot be written, which
※ is a thing the compiler can say only if it knows which was opened.  A fourth
※ kind is a fourth arm here and one more type a program may hand.  The numbers
※ handed to the runtime are the ways it knows: reading, writing and both.
@[export]
fn Dir.open(d: &Dir, name: str, kind: ⌜T'⌝) → T' ? i32
        pre(⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Reader⌝ ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Writer⌝ ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.ReadWriter⌝
            ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜Dir⌝):
    let bytes: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕bytes(name)
    let at: u64 = if #bytes = 0: 0u64 else: ⎕address(&bytes⟦0⟧)
    comptime if ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Reader⌝:
        let fd: i64 = io_open(0i32, d⌖.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: io.Reader(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d⌖.ring)
    comptime elif ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.Writer⌝:
        let fd: i64 = io_open(1i32, d⌖.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: io.Writer(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d⌖.ring)
    comptime elif ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜io.ReadWriter⌝:
        let fd: i64 = io_open(2i32, d⌖.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: io.ReadWriter(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d⌖.ring)
    else:
        ※ 3 is a directory, as the runtime numbers the ways
        let fd: i64 = io_open(3i32, d⌖.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: Dir(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d⌖.ring)

※※ Close a directory `Dir.open` opened, once every request in flight is answered.
@[export]
fn Dir.close(d: Dir):
    _ ← io_close(⎕at(d.ring, ⌜&mut io.Ring⌝), d.fd)
